{{- /*
=============================================================================
configure-pi-packages — idempotent ~/.pi/agent/settings.json packages patcher

Pi (the @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent open-source AI coding harness) reads
its installed package list from the `packages` array in
~/.pi/agent/settings.json. That file is NOT fully owned by the repo — pi
writes runtime keys (defaultProvider, defaultModel, defaultThinkingLevel,
lastChangelogVersion) and the user edits the file interactively via `pi
install`/`pi remove`. A templated file target would clobber those keys; this
jq merge preserves every existing field and only appends any specifiers from
.chezmoidata.yaml's `pi.packages` list that aren't already present.

Uses `run_onchange_before_` semantics:
  • `onchange_` runs only when THIS SCRIPT'S source content has changed
    (chezmoi state hash). The script body is rendered from
    `{{ range .pi.packages }}` so its hash changes whenever the inventory does
    — triggering a re-run exactly when the curated list moves.
  • `before_` runs during step 4 of the application order, before file
    targets are written — matters because ~/.pi/agent/settings.json is NOT a
    chezmoi-managed file; this script (alongside the theme script) is the only
    writer for the `packages` key.

Append-only contract. The jq patch:
  .packages as $have
  | ($want - $have) as $missing
  | .packages = ($have + $missing)
This *ensures presence* of the curated specifiers; it never removes entries.
Rationale: an interactive `pi install npm:foo` adds an entry the user chose;
`chezmoi apply` must not silently delete user choices from a file the user
co-owns. Removing a curated entry from .chezmoidata.yaml also leaves it on
disk — pruning requires `pi remove npm:<pkg>`. This mirrors the
docker-cli-plugins jq-merge pattern documented in AGENTS.md as the canonical
approach for target files the repo does not fully own.

No-op on a fresh machine where pi has never been run (the ~/.pi/agent/ dir
won't exist yet — pi creates it on first launch). The user runs `pi` once to
initialize, then re-runs `chezmoi apply` to install curated packages. Same
friendly-fail-mode pattern AGENTS.md describes for dot_zprofile and for the
pi theme script on a machine where pi isn't initialized yet.
=============================================================================
*/ -}}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

PI_DIR="$HOME/.pi/agent"
SETTINGS_FILE="$PI_DIR/settings.json"

# No-op if pi has never been run (dir or settings file doesn't exist).
if [ ! -f "$SETTINGS_FILE" ]; then
  echo "==> Pi settings not found at $SETTINGS_FILE — pi not initialized yet."
  echo "    Run 'pi' once to initialize, then re-run 'chezmoi apply' to install curated packages."
  exit 0
fi

# Desired specifier list, rendered by chezmoi at apply time from
# .chezmoidata.yaml's pi.packages section as a JSON array string.
WANT='{{ .pi.packages | toJson }}'

echo "==> Ensuring curated pi packages are present in $SETTINGS_FILE..."
tmp=$(mktemp)
jq --argjson want "$WANT" '
  ($want - (.packages // [])) as $missing
  | .packages = ((.packages // []) + $missing)
' "$SETTINGS_FILE" > "$tmp" && mv "$tmp" "$SETTINGS_FILE"

INSTALLED=$(jq -rc '.packages | join(", ")' "$SETTINGS_FILE")
echo "    packages = $INSTALLED (other keys preserved; interactive installs never removed)"
{{- else -}}
{{- /* No-op on non-macOS. */ -}}
{{- end -}}